title: Class steps and page objects
section: Writing tests
desc: Write steps as decorated classes, inject page objects and fixtures, and share one instance per scenario.
lede: If you prefer classes over functions, mark a class with @Steps() and decorate its methods. Page objects are plain classes that ask for what they need with inject().
---
<h2>A step class</h2>
<cb title="steps/login.steps.ts">import { Steps, Given, Then, inject, CONTEXT } from '@bddr/runtime';
import { LoginPage } from '../pages/login-page';

@Steps()
export class LoginSteps {
  private readonly login = inject(LoginPage);   // a page object
  private readonly page = inject('page');       // a fixture, by name
  private readonly ctx = inject(CONTEXT);       // the scenario context, if you need it

  @Given('I log in as {string}', { concurrent: false })
  async loginAs(user: string) {
    await this.login.as(user);
  }

  @Then('I see {string}')
  async seeText(text: string) {
    await this.page.getByText(text).waitFor();
  }
}</cb>
<ul>
  <li>Method arguments are the captured values only. The context is not passed in; ask for it with <code>inject(CONTEXT)</code>.</li>
  <li>Decorators take the same options as the function form: <code>{ timeoutMs, retry, concurrent, tags }</code>.</li>
  <li>The steps file only needs to be in the config's <code>steps</code> glob.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Page objects</h2>
<cb title="pages/login-page.ts">import { inject } from '@bddr/runtime';

export class LoginPage {
  private readonly page = inject('page');

  async as(user: string) {
    await this.page.goto('/login');
    await this.page.getByLabel('User').fill(user);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  }
}</cb>
<p>A class you inject is created once per scenario, and every step class that injects it gets the same instance. If it has a <code>dispose()</code> method, bddr calls it when the scenario ends.</p>
<aside class="callout note"><p class="callout-title">Constructors</p><p>When a class asks for an asynchronous fixture that is not ready yet, bddr creates the fixture and constructs the class again. Keep constructors to setting up fields, which is normal anyway.</p></aside>

<h2>Hooks in classes</h2>
<p>Decorators also work for hooks: on static methods for every level, and on instance methods for <code>Before</code>, <code>After</code>, <code>BeforeStep</code> and <code>AfterStep</code>.</p>
<cb>@Steps()
class Hooks {
  @Before({ name: 'open the home page' })
  async open() { /* ... */ }

  @After()
  async done() { /* ... */ }
}</cb>

<h2>Classes or functions?</h2>
<p>They match steps the same way and can live in the same project. Functions are shorter for small suites. Classes keep related steps and their page objects together when a suite grows. The <code>examples/playwright-showcase</code> and <code>examples/selenium-showcase</code> projects both use classes with page objects.</p>
